Return this JSON shape (omit any field you cannot determine):

{
  "description": "2–4 sentence catalogue description combining what is seen with what the provenance tells us.",
  "description_basis": "e.g. 'provenance caption + observation prose' or 'observation only'",
  "dense_summary": "≤ 400 chars. Entity-rich, no filler. Who/what/when/where.",
  "dense_summary_basis": "e.g. 'provenance caption and date field'",
  {% if not existingTitle %}
  "title": "Concise descriptive title.",
  "title_basis": "e.g. 'provenance caption' or 'derived from observation'",
  {% endif %}
  "date_hint": "Year, decade, or range e.g. '1965' or '1960s'",
  "date_confidence": 90,
  "date_basis": "e.g. 'provenance date field' or 'clothing style in observation'",
  "subjects": [{"term": "...", "confidence": 85, "basis": "short evidence string — always required"}],
  "people": [{"term": "Full Name or role description", "confidence": 70, "basis": "..."}],
  "places": [{"term": "Place name", "confidence": 95, "basis": "..."}],
  "organisations": [{"term": "Org name", "confidence": 80, "basis": "..."}],
  "language": "ISO 639-1 code if text is present — omit for non-text items"
}

Confidence is an integer 0–100. Always populate _basis fields — they are the audit trail distinguishing provenance-sourced facts from visual inference. "provenance caption" means the source caption stated it directly; "observation prose" means derived from what the AI saw; "provenance + observation" means both agree.
